          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I stopped my blood pressure tablets two months ago and my home readings are now around 155/95. Is that an emergency, and what does guidance describe about treatment and follow-up?",
          "answer": "Your blood pressure reading of 155/95 is above the typical threshold for hypertension, which is generally recognized as 140/90 mm Hg or higher. This situation may require attention, but it is not necessarily an immediate emergency unless you have severe symptoms. **If you have chest pain, severe headache, difficulty breathing, or changes in vision, seek emergency care right away.** \n\nAccording to guidance, after stopping blood pressure medication, it is important to have your blood pressure monitored regularly. Follow-up appointments should occur monthly until your target blood pressure is reached. Once stable, you may only need reviews every three to six months. The treatment approach may include lifestyle changes, such as diet and exercise, as well as medication if needed (guideline-hypertension-first-line-treatment, guideline-hypertension-diagnosis-and-bp-targets).\n\nSince you're noticing higher readings after stopping your medication, it would be wise to discuss your situation with your clinician, who can provide personalized guidance and support for managing your blood pressure. \n\nI could not provide specific medications or suggest particular lifestyle changes without considering your entire medical history, which should be discussed with your healthcare provider.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
